Difference Between controller and Restcontroller?

The main difference between @Controller and @RestController in Spring is how they handle HTTP responses and what kind of applications 
they are suited for:

@Controller
Used for traditional web applications where you want to return a view (like HTML, JSP, Thymeleaf, etc.) to the user.
Methods in a class annotated with @Controller typically return a view name that is resolved by a view resolver to generate the final HTML page.
If you want a method to return data (like JSON or XML) directly to the client, you must explicitly annotate the method with @ResponseBody. 
Without @ResponseBody, the return value is interpreted as a view name, not as data.

public class MyController {
    @RequestMapping("/hello")
    public String hello() {
        return "hello"; // returns view named "hello"
    }
    
    @RequestMapping("/data")
    @ResponseBody
    public MyData data() {
        return new MyData(); // returns JSON/XML
    }
}

@RestController

Designed specifically for RESTful web services.
It is a convenience annotation that combines @Controller and @ResponseBody.
All methods in a @RestController automatically serialize the return value (usually Java objects) to JSON or XML 
and write it directly to the HTTP response body—no need to add @ResponseBody to each method.
Does not support view resolution; always returns data, not views.

