what is circuite breaker pattern?

The Circuit Breaker Pattern is a resilience design pattern used in microservices and distributed systems to prevent cascading failures 
when one service depends on another.

How the Circuit Breaker Pattern Works
Monitoring: The circuit breaker monitors requests between services. If failures (such as timeouts or errors) exceed a certain threshold, 
the circuit breaker "trips."

States:
Closed: Requests flow normally. The circuit breaker monitors for failures.
Open: After too many failures, the circuit breaker opens, blocking further requests to the failing service for a set period. 
This gives the service time to recover and prevents further strain.
Half-Open: After the timeout, the circuit breaker allows a limited number of test requests to check if the service has recovered. 
If successful, it returns to the closed state; if not, it goes back to open

@CircuitBreaker(name = "myService", fallbackMethod = "fallbackMethod")
public String callServiceB() {
    return restTemplate.getForObject("http://service-b/data", String.class);
}

public String fallbackMethod(Throwable t) {
    return "Service B is currently unavailable. Please try later.";
}

