What is @Transactional in Spring Boot?

The @Transactional annotation in Spring Boot (and Spring Framework) is used to manage database transactions automatically. 
It marks a method or class so that all DB operations inside it are treated as a single atomic unit.
If any part of the transaction fails, the entire operation is rolled back

| Propagation          | Meaning                                        |
| -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| `REQUIRED` (default) | Join existing transaction or create new one    |
| `REQUIRES_NEW`       | Always start a **new** transaction             |
| `NESTED`             | Nested transaction with savepoints             |
| `SUPPORTS`           | Use transaction if exists; else no transaction |
| `NOT_SUPPORTED`      | Suspend transaction if one exists              |
| `NEVER`              | Throw exception if transaction exists          |
| `MANDATORY`          | Must run inside a transaction                  |
 
🛑 Important Tips
Transactional only works on public methods.
It won't work on internal method calls (like this.method() inside the same class).
Works best with Spring-managed beans (i.e., components/services injected via @Autowired or @Component).

