difference between reactive programming and non reactive programming?

🟩 Reactive Programming:
A programming paradigm focused on asynchronous, non-blocking data streams and event-driven systems.
Built on Publisher–Subscriber model
Uses reactive streams: Flux, Mono
Efficient for high concurrency & I/O-bound operations

🟥 Non-Reactive (Imperative) Programming:
Traditional way of programming where execution is sequential, often blocking, and request/response is synchronous.
Uses standard Java types like List, Optional
Each thread waits for task to complete (blocking I/O)

| Aspect        | Reactive                 | Non-Reactive                   |
| ------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------------ |
| Blocking      | ❌ Non-blocking           | ✅ Blocking                     |
| Thread Usage  | Efficient, event-loop    | Thread-per-request model       |
| Data Handling | Streams (`Mono`, `Flux`) | Regular types (`List`, `User`) |
| Complexity    | Higher learning curve    | Easier to understand           |
| Performance   | Scales better under load | Simple and direct              |

